  "worked_solution": "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x{e}^{y}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ [(x)({e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+(1)({e}^{y})] & =2x \\\\ x\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x \\\\ x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-{e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
